Z||000006||lou_dias||01-26-2001||09:48 AM||||I had a groved drilled into my lower intake at both ends so that when you put the RTV there and the engine heats up and that stuff expands, it has a groove to hold itself into place and avert any oil leaks there||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||01-26-2001||01:39 PM||||Lance,<P>The front main seal is the seal right behind the v-belt pulley on the end of the crankshaft. It mounts into the timing chain cover.<P>The 2.5 is notorious for leaking here. Part of the problem is the timing chain cover doesn't have pins to hold it in an exact position. When installing the timing chain cover with a new seal, great care must be taken to ensure the seal is centered on the crankshaft.<P>Also, the front pulley (harmonic balancer) will usually have a groove worn in it by the old seal. You can buy a sleeve to slip over it and give a smooth sealing surface when installing the new seal.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||01-26-2001||03:50 PM||||Check and see if its your oilpan also, i done mine about 3 times.  its the same car as yours.  <P>if your in a shopclass, that would be the best place to fix it because they have a engine hoist.<P>The two sides of the pan are the places where they tend to leak, especially by the timing cover.  If you want to try to do your pan, it is a 2-4 hour job.  Alternator removal, starter, engine mount, and finally the pan.  ||||reg||1
